Pinned anonymous fork density and pin isolation
=============================================

Build pinned-fork with the other arm64/user4k tests. Run as root through the
established 4K userspace launcher on a native16K kernel:

  userpages 4k ./pinned-fork --native-16k --expect-packed

The fixture initializes one native-aligned1MiB anonymous range, registers real
writable long-term io_uring buffer pins, then forks while the parent retains the
registration. It requires every copied child native PFN to be disjoint from the
pinned parent's native PFNs. It checks every byte before and after distinct
parent/child writes; registration and PFN disjointness, not mlock or VmPin alone,
establish copy-path coverage. Kernel/pin-policy limitations return77 UNEXERCISED.

--expect-packed requires child backing <=1.25MiB for1MiB copied data, with bounded
pool-shard slack. Without this option the fixture also accepts the old4MiB
backing so a baseline can record both content and density separately. Successful
contents alone do not establish packed density. --selftest checks metadata PFN
grouping only, with no dynamic pin/fork qualification.

Optional --hold reports the parent PID, child PID and range base after fork PFN
verification, then waits for the byte 'c' on stdin. A controller can hold both
processes in one fresh uncapped non-root cgroup and verify exact payload charges:
read each target's pagemap in4KiB units; normalize PFN>>2 to native16KiB PFN;
read /proc/kpagecgroup at native_PFN*8 and require each returned inode equals
stat(the fixture cgroup).st_ino. Re-read pagemap to reject migration during the
sample. This directly catches incorrectly root-charged fork copies; total
memory.current includes unrelated fixture code/stack/table costs and is not an
exact payload charge oracle. Verify both PIDs belong to the same owned cgroup,
record memory.stat/status, and release promptly (within3seconds).

Parent15s/child10s alarms bound the protocol; parent exit kills/reaps the child
and the child uses PDEATHSIG. Payload native backing is at most5MiB on the old
path. No forced reclaim, memory.max/high changes, GPU operations, or swap-area
changes are used. Controllers should additionally enforce their own process-group
or service-unit lifetime and preserve logs on any failure.
